WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:11.400
In this lesson, you will learn to use traversal to calculate an average and find the maximum and minimum values in a list.

2
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.843
Average = total ÷ number of items.

3
00:00:15.843 --> 00:00:21.426
Traverse to get the total, then divide by length of list.

4
00:00:22.026 --> 00:00:25.000
Start max as item 1.

5
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.583
Traverse: if item i > max, set max to item i.

6
00:00:30.583 --> 00:00:34.861
At the end, max holds the biggest value.

7
00:00:35.461 --> 00:00:39.304
Same idea with < instead of >.

8
00:00:39.304 --> 00:00:45.757
Start min as item 1, not 0 — otherwise 0 might win wrongly.

9
00:00:46.357 --> 00:00:54.548
Averages and extremes turn raw numbers into facts you can act on: “Saturday was the hottest day.”

10
00:00:55.148 --> 00:01:04.209
Python has built-in sum, max and min — but inside they work exactly like the loops you are writing.

11
00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:08.652
Great work! Let’s remember what we learned.

12
00:01:08.652 --> 00:01:13.800
Now build along, then try the challenges. You’ve got this!
